    <string name="guide_food_rules_title">Healthy Food Intake Rules</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s6_title">Fruit Intake Rules</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s6_content">Fruits are rich in vitamins but also contain sugar.\n\n• Eat whole fruits rather than juice.\n• Do not eat large amounts at once.\n• Be careful with dried or canned fruits as sugar is concentrated.\n• Eating too much fruit as dessert significantly increases total carb intake.</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s7_title">Understanding GI and GL</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s7_content">GI (Glycemic Index) indicates the speed of glucose rise, while GL (Glycemic Load) considers both GI and actual portion size.\n\nEven low GI foods can raise glucose if eaten in large amounts. Don\'t judge based on GI numbers alone; check your personal response.</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s8_title">Dining Out Tips</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s8_content">• Adjust portions of rice or noodles in advance.\n• Choose grilled, steamed, or boiled options over fried.\n• Ask for sauces and dressings on the side.\n• Eat plenty of vegetable side dishes and less soup.</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s9_title">Wise Snack Choices</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s9_content">Check your hunger and glucose before snacking. Recommendations:\n\n• Unsweetened yogurt, small portions of nuts, boiled eggs, vegetable sticks.\n\nBe aware that even healthy snacks increase total calories and carbs.</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s10_title">Drinks and Alcohol</string>
    <string name="guide_f_s10_content">Water is the most basic hydration. Zero-sugar drinks help reduce sugar intake but shouldn\'t completely replace water.\n\nAlcohol can raise glucose or cause delayed hypoglycemia, especially with insulin. Don\'t drink on an empty stomach.</string>

